+++ b/modules/ensemble_indicators.py
@@ -0,0 +1,868 @@
+"""
+Ensemble indicators for posterior ensembles of 3D labelled models: cell-wise
+uncertainty maps, comparison of groups of models cell by cell, connectivity,
+directional variograms and per-model descriptors. Adapted from loopUI
+(Pirot et al., 2022) to trans-dimensional (trans-D) level-set ensembles.
+
+Credit
+------
+The indicators follow loopUI-0.1 (Pirot, Joshi, Giraud, Lindsay & Jessell,
+2022; https://github.com/Loop3D/loopUI, MIT licence):
+
+    this module                       loopUI-0.1 (loopui.py)
+    -------------------------------   -------------------------------------------
+    cardinality_map                   cardinality
+    entropy_map                       entropy
+    js_divergence_map                 kldiv(..., divtype='js'), jsdist_hist
+    connectivity_gamma, gamma_curves  continuous_pct_connectivity
+    lag_statistics: tau_k(h)          indicator_lag_connectivity
+    lag_statistics: gamma_k(h)        experimental_variogram (categorical)
+    block_distance_correlation        comparison of indicators (ui-9-comparison)
+
+as does the distinction between local indicators (one value per cell, cost
+linear in the number of models N) and global ones (one value per model or per
+pair of models). The functions are re-implemented, not copied from loopUI, and
+differ from it in five respects that matter for posterior ensembles:
+
+1. Unit identity. loopUI takes the distinct values of a voxet as categories,
+   which assumes that the models share their stratigraphy. In trans-D
+   ensembles the unit densities are sampled, so that one unit carries a
+   different value in each model, and units are born and die. The indicators
+   are computed on canonical unit codes instead: each model is matched to a
+   reference model by motif_analysis.canonical_labels (label switching;
+   Stephens, 2000), and all born units are grouped in one class
+   (canonical_ensemble).
+2. Groups. Cell-wise indicators are computed per group of models (chain,
+   cluster, prior or posterior ensemble), and groups are compared cell by cell
+   (js_divergence_map, kl_divergence_map) rather than model by model.
+3. Per-model descriptors instead of pairwise distances. loopUI compares models
+   two by two, N(N - 1)/2 comparisons (about 2e6 for N = 2000). Here the
+   connectivity and variogram curves are computed once per model and
+   concatenated in a descriptor vector (descriptor_table), at a cost linear
+   in N; the vectors can be appended to the features of ensemble_features and
+   embedded or clustered with them.
+4. Exact directional statistics. loopUI draws random pairs of cells and pools
+   all directions. Here every pair of cells at lag h along one grid axis is
+   used (no sampling), separately along depth, y and x: layered models are
+   anisotropic, and an omnidirectional curve averages the vertical and lateral
+   behaviours.
+5. Shared thresholds. loopUI thresholds each continuous model at its own
+   percentiles; gamma_curves uses thresholds common to all models, in density
+   units, so that the curves of all models describe the same sets of cells
+   (e.g. "denser than 2900 kg/m3").
+
+Not carried over: the multiple-point histogram and wavelet distances overlap
+with the fixed-kernel encoder of ensemble_features and with the Haralick
+features, and the former needs two k-means fits per pair of models; the
+topological distances of loopUI work on the unit-level adjacency matrix,
+which the topology key and the body graph of motif_analysis already contain.
+
+Conventions (as in motif_analysis.py)
+-------------------------------------
+- models: ndarray (nz, ny, nx, N) of densities, model i = models[..., i];
+  axis 0 is depth, index 0 at the top.
+- codes: ndarray (nz, ny, nx, N) of canonical codes (uint8): 0..K-1 are the
+  reference units ordered by increasing mean depth (0 = top, K-1 = deepest),
+  codes >= K the born units of each model (distinct within a model, not
+  across models). Every function groups the codes >= K in one class K, the
+  born class, so that n_classes = K + 1; within a model, the bodies of
+  different born units remain distinct, as in motif_analysis.
+- p: class frequencies with the class axis fourth from the end,
+  (n_classes, nz, ny, nx) for one group or (G, n_classes, nz, ny, nx) for G
+  groups; p[..., k, iz, iy, ix] is the fraction of the models of a group in
+  which cell (iz, iy, ix) belongs to class k.
+- 6-connectivity (shared faces) for bodies and connected components.
+- Entropies and divergences in nats (natural logarithm).
+
+Contents
+--------
+0. canonical_ensemble                      codes of every model
+1. class_counts, class_frequencies,        per-group frequencies of each class at each cell,
+   pooled_frequencies                      and of all groups together
+   entropy_map, cardinality_map            local uncertainty (loopUI)
+   js_divergence_map, js_null_level        cell-wise disagreement between groups
+   effective_sample_size,                  effective sample size of a sequence, and of each chain
+   chain_effective_sizes                   (for the reference level of the disagreement)
+   kl_divergence_map                       cell-wise information gain, prior -> posterior
+   group_summary                           one row of summary statistics per group
+2. connectivity_gamma, gamma_curves        connectivity of density sets (percolation)
+   class_densities                         density ranges of the classes, to place thresholds
+   connected_between, box                  hypothesis "regions A and B are connected"
+3. lag_statistics, class_gamma             directional indicator variograms and
+                                           connectivity functions of the classes
+4. descriptor_table, descriptor_names      per-model descriptor vectors
+5. block_distance_correlation              redundancy between descriptor blocks
+6. plot_sections                           map and two sections of a cell-wise volume
+
+Usage
+-----
+    import ensemble_indicators as ei
+    codes, n_cl = ei.canonical_ensemble(core, ref)                  # ref: reference codes
+    p, names, n = ei.class_frequencies(codes, n_cl, groups=chain_id)
+    H = ei.entropy_map(p)                                           # (G, nz, ny, nx)
+    p_all = ei.pooled_frequencies(p, n)                             # all chains together
+    neff = ei.chain_effective_sizes(codes, n_cl, chain_id, reference=ref)  # N_eff of each chain
+    JS = ei.js_divergence_map(p, weights=n)                         # chains disagree where JS >> JS0
+    JS0 = ei.js_null_level(p_all, n, neff['N_eff'])
+    g, frac = ei.gamma_curves(core, thresholds=[2700, 2850, 2950])  # (N, T)
+    X = ei.descriptor_table(codes, n_cl)                            # DataFrame (N, F)
+
